{
 "type": "instrument",
 "id": "guitar",
 "name": "Nylon-string guitar",
 "version": 1,
 "note": "An instrument recipe for the sampler (doc 24). A classical (nylon-string) guitar, plucked, not looped. Each file is one pluck cut from the Iowa MIS chromatic runs: trimmed to 3 ms before the onset, resampled from 96 kHz to 48 kHz, cut at the next pluck less 20 ms or at 4 s, whichever comes first, with a 250 ms equal-power fade to silence, as the harp. No gain change. Mono. formats: <file>.ogg is Ogg Opus 56 kbps mono (variable bitrate), <file>.m4a is AAC-LC 80 kbps mono. Zone fields as in harp.json: measuredCents is the zone's pitch against 12-TET at A4 = 440 Hz, fitted from partials 1 to 6 of the louder layer; tuneConfidence 0 to 1; string and fret say where it was played. layers: v1 is pp, v2 is mf (the library's ff is not used: the game plays the guitar softly). Tools: tools/audio-prep/guitar/ (prep.py, build.py).",
 "source": {
  "library": "University of Iowa Electronic Music Studios, Musical Instrument Samples (MIS): Guitar",
  "url": "https://theremin.music.uiowa.edu/MISguitar.html",
  "license": "Free to use: the studio says the recordings \"may be downloaded and used for any projects, without restrictions\". Not formally CC0, so listed in assets/audio/LICENSES-NONCC0.md (doc 25).",
  "notes": "Raimundo 118 classical guitar, played by Brian Penkrot (2011); Earthworks QTC40 microphone at 5 ft, anechoic chamber; mono 24-bit 96 kHz AIFF (Guitar.mono.2496.zip). One file per string per dynamic (pp, mf, ff), each a chromatic run."
 },
